Ethernet: collection of network protocols/standards

UTP cables use 4 twisted pairs (8 pins), with pins 1–2 and 3–6 being the pairs used for 10/100 Mbps Ethernet.

Full-duplex means sending and receiving simultaneously.

Traditionally, PCs, routers, and firewalls use the same pin assignment (1–2 = TX, 3–6 = RX), while switches use the opposite (1–2 = RX, 3–6 = TX). Therefore, a
straight-through cable is used between different interface types (e.g., PC → switch), while a crossover cable swaps 1–2 ↔ 3–6 and is traditionally used between the
same interface types (e.g., PC → PC, PC → router, router → router, firewall → router).

Auto MDI-X, found on most modern Ethernet devices, automatically detects the TX/RX configuration and electronically adjusts it, meaning either a straight-through or
crossover cable can usually be used, eliminating the need to manually choose the correct cable.
